- what is a sorting algo?
    A sorting algorithm is a step-by-step procedure used in computer science to rearrange a collection of data (like an array or a list) into a specific, structured order. Most commonly, this means putting numbers in numerical order (e.g., 1 to 10) or text in alphabetical/lexicographical order. [1] Organizing data makes it significantly cleaner for humans to read and allows other software programs—like search tools and databases—to run exponentially faster. 
- why we use these algo?
    We use sorting algorithms because computers and humans can process, search, and analyze organized data drastically faster than unstructured data. In computer science, disorder is incredibly expensive; when data is randomized, every downstream operation requires more computational effort. 
